xc benchmark visualiser @VERSION@

Draws a benchmark contribution (the JSON benchmark/diy.py writes) and the
project's published results, compares compilers in a heat map, and moves
contributions to and from the public corpus.

Run it:

  ./visualiser                  an empty window: Download shows the published
                                results and everyone's submitted runs
  ./visualiser results.json     open your own contribution
  ./visualiser --selftest sample.json
                                check the program without a window

On macOS the archive is not notarised. If macOS refuses to open it, run
`xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine .` in this directory once.

On Linux the machine needs GTK 4 (libgtk-4-1 on Ubuntu), and the self-test
needs a display too (xvfb-run works on a machine without one).

On Windows run visualiser.exe the same way, from a command prompt in this
folder. The program is new and not signed, so on the first run Windows may
say it "protected your PC"; choose More info, then Run anyway. https
certificates are checked against the Windows certificate store.

The libraries beside the program are libtls (Mbed TLS, Apache-2.0 or
GPL-2.0-or-later, LICENSE-mbedtls.txt) and, on macOS and Linux, the toolkit's
shim (libUXAppKit.dylib or libUXGtk.so, LGPL-3.0, COPYING.LESSER). The program
is GPL-3.0 (COPYING).

Guides: https://compile-xc.org/compiler/benchmarks/
